  "text": "Let's solve this problem step by step:\n\nGiven:\n\\[ n + 2 \\mid 3(n + 3)(n^2 + 9) \\]\n\nWe need to find the sum of all positive integers \\( n \\) that satisfy this condition.\n\n### Step 1: Understanding the condition\nFor \\( n + 2 \\) to divide \\( 3(n + 3)(n^2 + 9) \\), it must be that \\( n + 2 \\) is a divisor of \\( 3(n + 3)(n^2 + 9) \\).\n\n### Step 2: Analyzing the expression\nWe need to find \\( n \\) such that \\( n + 2 \\) is a divisor of \\( 3(n + 3)(n^2 + 9) \\).\n\nLet's denote:\n\\[ k = n + 2 \\]\n\nThen:\n\\[ n = k - 2 \\]\n\nSubstitute \\( n \\) into the expression:\n\\[ 3(n + 3)(n^2 + 9) = 3((k - 2) + 3)((k - 2)^2 + 9) \\]\n\nSimplify:\n\\[ = 3(k + 1)((k - 2)^2 + 9) \\]\n\nExpand \\( (k - 2)^2 \\):\n\\[ (k - 2)^2 = k^2 - 4k + 4 \\]\n\nSo:\n\\[ (k - 2)^2 + 9 = k^2 - 4k + 4 + 9 = k^2 - 4k + 13 \\]\n\nThus, the expression becomes:\n\\[ 3(k + 1)(k^2 - 4k + 13) \\]\n\n### Step 3: Finding the divisors\nWe need \\( k \\) (which is \\( n + 2 \\)) to be a divisor of \\( 3(k + 1)(k^2 - 4k + 13) \\).\n\nSince \\( k \\) is a divisor of \\( 3(k + 1)(k^2 - 4k + 13) \\), we can write:\n\\[ k \\mid 3(k + 1)(k^2 - 4k + 13) \\]\n\nThis means:\n\\[ k \\mid 3(k + 1) \\quad \\text{or} \\quad k \\mid (k^2 - 4k + 13) \\]\n\n### Step 4: Analyzing the divisors\n#### Case 1: \\( k \\mid 3(k + 1) \\)\nIf \\( k \\mid 3(k + 1) \\), then \\( k \\) must divide 3. So, \\( k \\) can be 1, 3, or 9.\n\n#### Case 2: \\( k \\mid (k^2 - 4k + 13) \\)\nWe need to check if \\( k \\) divides \\( k^2 - 4k + 13 \\).\n\nFor \\( k = 1 \\):\n\\[ 1^2 - 4(1) + 13 = 1 - 4 + 13 = 10 \\]\n\\[ 1 \\mid 10 \\quad \\text{(True)} \\]\n\nFor \\( k = 3 \\):\n\\[ 3^2 - 4(3) + 13 = 9 - 12 + 13 = 10 \\]\n\\[ 3 \\mid 10 \\quad \\text{(False)} \\]\n\nFor \\( k = 9 \\):\n\\[ 9^2 - 4(9) + 13 = 81 - 36 + 13 = 58 \\]\n\\[ 9 \\mid 58 \\quad \\text{(False)} \\]\n\n### Step 5: Summarizing the valid \\( k \\)\nThe valid values of \\( k \\) are 1, 3, and 9.\n\n### Step 6: Finding the corresponding \\( n \\)\nIf \\( k = 1 \\):\n\\[ n = k - 2 = 1 - 2 = -1 \\quad \\text{(Invalid, since \\( n \\) must be positive)} \\]\n\nIf \\( k = 3 \\):\n\\[ n = k - 2 = 3 - 2 = 1 \\]\n\nIf \\( k = 9 \\):\n\\[ n = k - 2 = 9 - 2 = 7 \\]\n\n### Step 7: Summing the valid \\( n \\)\nThe valid \\( n \\) values are 1 and 7. Their sum is:\n\\[ 1 + 7 = 8 \\]\n\n### Final Answer\n\\(\\boxed{8}\\)",
